riscv64 zkVM: unrolled block copies as the ZisK DMA memcpy idiom

A struct copy or a constant-size block copy was unrolled into a load and a store per
eight bytes: two steps per word on the zkVM, where the 32-byte copies of UInt256 and
Hash256 alone were 3.3% of all executed instructions in the traced Nethermind block
(4.4% for all unrolled copies). The ZisK DMA precompile (perf-56/58 for memmove
P/Invokes) copies any length in two instructions: `csrrs x0, 0x813, <source>; add x0,
<destination>, <length>`. JitZkMemcpyCsr=<bytes> (default 0 = off; bflat passes 0x10)
emits an unrolled GT_STORE_BLK copy of at least that many bytes as `[lea/addi source;]
[lea/addi destination;] li length; csrrs; add` - three to five instructions - with LSRA
reserving the length register and one per address to materialize. Address modes with
an index or an offset outside simm12, unaligned copies in strict-alignment mode and the
CpObj (GC write barrier) kind keep their stores.

Measured on nine mainnet blocks on top of perf-62..64: -2.44% executed steps
(1,463,698,587 = -5.07% versus perf-61, -14.08% versus master); 16- and 32-byte
minimums are the same to 0.004%. Outputs identical.

